<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">836756</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2025316</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>A cell line from an anaplastic transitional cell carcinoma of human urinary bladder.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nayak</surname><given-names>S. K.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>O'Toole</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Price</surname><given-names>Z. H.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>2</month><year>1977</year></pub-date><volume>35</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>142</fpage><lpage>151</lpage><abstract><p>A cell line, TCCSUP, derived from an undifferentiated, Grade IV transitional cell carcinoma is described. The karyotype showed an abnormal distribution of chromosomes, with no obvious modal number. Distinct marker chromosomes were observed in both early and late in vitro passages. These cells have been subcultured over 50 times during a 20-month period. TCCSUP differs in certain morphological and immunological features from other cell lines from transitional cell carcinomas.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>Fig. 1</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0014-a" xlink:role="144"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>Fig. 2</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0014-b" xlink:role="144"/></fig><fig id="F3"><label>Fig. 3</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0015-a" xlink:role="145"/></fig><fig id="F4"><label>Fig. 4</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0016-a" xlink:role="146"/></fig><fig id="F5"><label>Fig. 5</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0017-a" xlink:role="147"/></fig><fig id="F6"><label>Fig. 6</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0018-a" xlink:role="148"/></fig><fig id="F7"><label>Fig. 7</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0018-b" xlink:role="148"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0012.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="142" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0013.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="143" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0014.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="144" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0015.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="145" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0016.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="146" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0017.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="147" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0018.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="148" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0019.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="149" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0020.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="150" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00299-0021.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="151" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>